  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "The sum of the squares of the roots of the equation \\(x^2+2hx=3\\) is \\(10.\\) The absolute value of \\(h\\) is equal to:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(-1\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(\\frac{1}{2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(\\frac{2}{3}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "None of these"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "e",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1971-p20.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "For roots \\(r,s,\\) Vieta’s formulas give \\(r+s=-2h\\) and \\(rs=-3.\\) Hence \\[ \\begin{aligned} 4h^2=(r+s)^2 &=r^2+s^2+2rs\\\\ &=10-6=4. \\end{aligned} \\] Therefore \\(|h|=1,\\) which is not among choices A-D because choice A is \\(-1.\\)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is E.",
      "hints": [
        "Use the sum and product of the two roots",
        "If the roots are \\(r,s,\\) then \\(r+s=-2h\\) and \\(rs=-3\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
